How do you define tactics as being different from strategy?  Aside
from the most obvious implications of melee combat, naturally.

>I've tried it that way and learned my lesson.  I figure you'll have to
>use savescumming for a year or so and discover it isn't working any
>more.  Because it is incapable of teaching the parts of the game that
>are interesting.

And what precisely would you identify as "the parts of the game that
are interesting" - parts that savescumming necessarily removes from
the game?  As I see it, savescumming merely removes the risk inherent
at any given moment.  Although I see your argument that suggests that
a far distant decision might influence immediate gameplay (i.e.,
advancing one class over another), I would think that such
implications from decision-making are present in all similar startegic
games, so there is nothing unique here.  I don't think it's fair to
suggest that savescumming could override the necessary consequences of
a distant decision, since so much goes into *any* one given decision.
It would be unfair to ascribe that much significance to any one
choice.  

The commonly-read argument that savescumming simply "encourages
players to lack imagination, allowing them to use the same strategy
over and over until it manages to work" is utterly spurious to me.  In
fact, it is precisely the opposite that using save-states affords, it
actually allows the player to experiment with different approaches,
learning what works and what doesn't and why.  Only the crudest-minded
player would use savestates to hammer a losing technique over and over
I think.   


